// A re-exported fold reached through its module's qualifier from three places in
// the load order: from a module loaded before the folding one, from a module
// loaded after it, and from the root, which loads first of all. One answer three
// times.
//
// The companion of module-nameres-transit.cone, which reaches the same names
// through modulehide -- the same file with the re-export left off -- and is
// refused everywhere. Between them they say that what decides the reach is the
// binding the fold made, and nothing else.
//
// WHAT USED TO BE PINNED HERE, AND IS FIXED: these three positions gave three
// answers. A module's folds ran at the start of that module's own name
// resolution and modules were resolved in the order they were loaded, so the
// root found nothing, a module loaded earlier found nothing, and one loaded
// later found the names. Every module's folds now run before any module's body
// is resolved, dependency-first, so the position in the order decides nothing.
//
// 'import moduleearly' is what makes moduleearly early: it is added to the
// program when its parse begins, ahead of its own import of moduletransit.
// modulelate is imported after moduletransit and is therefore late.
//
// It compiles rather than runs for the reason every 'import' scenario does: the
// imported modules' bodies are not generated here. cases.toml pins what this
// file declares for the names the three reaches lowered to.
//
// The imported modules are moduleearly.cone, moduletransit.cone, modulelate.cone
// and, through moduletransit, modulesub.cone.

import moduleearly;
import moduletransit;
import modulelate;

fn viaEitherSide() i64 {
  moduleearly.viaEarly(1i64) + modulelate.viaMid(2i64);
}

// The root's own reach, which is the half that could not be written before: the
// root is resolved first, so a name one of its imports had folded was not there
// yet whatever its visibility
fn viaRoot() i64 {
  moduletransit.plain(3i64) + moduletransit.scale(4i64) + moduletransit.midOwn(5i64);
}
